 "notes": "The list is on n51.jpg (p. 40, 33 rows, Longstone to South Sand Head) and n52.jpg (p. 41, 5 rows, East Side to Hanois). n50, n53 and n54 do not have it: I opened n53, which is the species notes, and did not open n50 or n54. That makes 38 stations: 25 starred and 13 without a star (Spurn L.V., Llyn Wells, Hasbro' L.H., Galloper, Swin Middle, Tongue, Nore, North Foreland, South Sand Head, South Foreland, Casquets, and on n51 no others). All 13 unstarred rows have no keeper; every starred row names one or more.\n\nFormat: 'L.H.' and 'L.V.' are printed in small capitals (l.h., l.v.); I wrote them in upper case. Leader dots between columns are shown as a single '...' in 'printed' and are left out of every other field. Where the type runs into the leaders ('L.V....', 'L.H....') I kept the run of dots as printed. Nothing in the list is in italics, and no numbers or light characters are given.\n\nMarks: '*' means the station made returns. Longstone also carries '†', the footnote '† For nature of light, position, and distance from the nearest land, see report for 1880.' The section heading 'EAST COAST OF ENGLAND.' appears only as the running head on p. 41; the list has no sub-headings. Casquets and Hanois (Channel Islands) are still listed under this coast.\n\nKeepers: where a row joins names with '&' or commas, I split it into one entry per person and kept each fragment exactly as printed. The full stop that ends the row stays on the last name. This matters for two rows. 'J.N.Utting' is printed with no spaces. Hanois's 'Geo. Freeman, C. Williams, & F. G. Cutting.' gives three entries; the comma before '&' is in the print.\n\nSpellings copied as printed, which you may want to normalise in the table:\n- 'Llyn Wells' is Lynn Well L.V.\n- 'Languard Point' is Landguard Point. The intro paragraph spells it 'Languard' too.\n- 'Hasbro'' is Happisburgh. The species notes call it 'Hasborough'.\n- 'Orfordness' is printed as one word.\n- 'W, Elsdon' at Kentish Knock has a comma where a full stop is meant.\n- 'Edward le Gallais' has a lower-case 'le'.\n\nOCR errors I corrected from the images: 'Vlamborough' is Flamborough, '§purn' is Spurn, 'xNewarp' is *Newarp, 'Bare. Live jin f' is 'Nore L.V.', 'South Sand Hears' is South Sand Head, 'Hast Side' is East Side, '.. od. Jacobs' is J. Jacobs, and the Hanois keepers garbled as 'Gan, Beco G. William!' read 'Geo. Freeman, C. Williams'. The OCR's '- South Foreland' has no star in the print. The keeper column on p. 40 lines up row by row with the stations in the image. The OCR's separate keeper block cannot be paired by counting, because the rows without keepers leave gaps.\n\nOther marks: there is a small hand-made tick after the leaders on the Nore row and a speck after Owen Boyle's row. Both are on the scan, not in the type. Small specks over 'Francis', in 'J. ·Rees' and on the C of 'Casquets' are also blemishes, not letters.\n\nCross-references: the paragraph just above the list names Thomas H. Cutting (Inner Farn, nine schedules) and Owen Boyle (Languard, four schedules). A footnote on p. 42 names 'Mr. T. O. Hall' of Longstone. A 'Cutting' also appears at Hanois (F. G. Cutting), which may be worth checking against Thomas H. Cutting in the keeper roll.",
